---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What are the main parts of CELPIP Reading?",
    answer:
      "CELPIP Reading includes correspondence, diagram-based reading, information reading, and viewpoints reading.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to read every CELPIP passage slowly from beginning to end?",
    answer:
      "No. Some questions reward quick scanning, while others need slower reading to compare ideas and viewpoints carefully.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the biggest reading mistake in CELPIP?",
    answer:
      "A common mistake is choosing an answer that sounds reasonable but is not fully supported by the passage.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "CELPIP Reading Question Types Guide", path: "/blog/celpip-reading-question-types/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="CELPIP Reading Question Types Guide | Read Faster and Choose Better"
  description="A practical CELPIP Reading guide with section-by-section strategy for correspondence, diagrams, information passages, and viewpoints."
  type="article"
  keywords="CELPIP reading question types, CELPIP reading strategy, CELPIP reading tips"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">CELPIP reading guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">CELPIP Reading question types</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        CELPIP Reading is not only about speed. It is also about reading with the right
        purpose. Some parts want quick practical information, while others test whether you
        can compare opinions carefully.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once you know what each reading part is really testing, your choices become much
        more accurate.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card">
      <h2>The main reading parts</h2>
      <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Reading correspondence</h3>
          <p class="muted">Focus on purpose, request, reply, and practical details.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Reading to apply a diagram</h3>
          <p class="muted">Match the text to a visual plan or process carefully.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Reading for information</h3>
          <p class="muted">Track facts, sequence, and the meaning of specific details.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Reading for viewpoints</h3>
          <p class="muted">Compare attitudes, arguments, and points of disagreement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>How to read each type better</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li><strong>Correspondence:</strong> find who wants what, and why.</li>
        <li><strong>Diagram tasks:</strong> move between the text and the visual, not only the passage.</li>
        <li><strong>Information passages:</strong> scan first, then read the key area more closely.</li>
        <li><strong>Viewpoints:</strong> separate each person's opinion before choosing an answer.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>What usually causes wrong answers</h2>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>Problem</th>
          <th>Better move</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The answer sounds possible, but the passage does not fully support it.</td>
          <td>Choose only what the text really says or clearly implies.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Students read too slowly in the early questions.</td>
          <td>Scan first, then slow down only where the answer is likely to be.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Different viewpoints get mixed together.</td>
          <td>Mark each speaker's or writer's opinion clearly.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The visual part of a diagram task is ignored.</td>
          <td>Use the text and the diagram together.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>A simple reading habit that helps</h2>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          Before choosing an answer, ask: <strong>Where is the proof?</strong> This one
          question improves reading accuracy more than guessing from general meaning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/blog/celpip-listening-question-types">
          <strong>Read the CELPIP listening guide</strong>
          <span>Pair your reading strategy with the same section-by-section approach in listening.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/celpip/reading">
          <strong>Go to the CELPIP reading hub</strong>
          <span>Open the main reading page for more strategy and resources.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/celpip/reading">
          <strong>Study CELPIP reading lessons</strong>
          <span>Practice the vocabulary and reading habits behind these question types.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <section class="section-sm" style="max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center;">
    <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; margin-bottom: var(--s-5);" />
    <p class="muted">Want a CLB 9 writing system you can actually follow?</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/celpip/writing">Get the system</a>
  </section>
</Layout>
